title: Styles
pid: 6ee2710f-a26c-469e-880f-597dcbcdb8b4
body: |2-
  A herald's style states its importance tier, or for a few heralds a
  polarity or kind, never its hue. There are twelve styles.

  | Style       | Look                           | Meaning                         |
  |-------------+--------------------------------+---------------------------------|
  | crucial     | black on cyan                  | the reader must understand this |
  | high        | black on yellow                | high interest                   |
  | medium      | white on orange                | medium interest                 |
  | low         | white on olive                 | low interest                    |
  | normal      | white on blue                  | ordinary                        |
  | message     | black on white                 | prose meant to be read          |
  | stop        | white on red                   | polarity: bad, or not yet       |
  | go          | black on green                 | polarity: good, done            |
  | nonstandard | white on purple                | requests and change facts       |
  | ugly        | pink foreground, no background | error-flavored, but not too bad |
  | boring      | white on dark gray             | counts seldom worth reading     |
  | ghost       | ghost-white on dark slate blue | kind: what HEAD alone warrants  |

  The tiers, least to greatest, are normal, low, medium, high and crucial.

  Each style has one name everywhere. The looks are defined in
  [[file:../shared/herald-styles.json][shared/herald-styles.json]], which both clients read. That file also
  says which style each herald takes: 'relationship_heralds' for the
  relationship heralds, which the clients render themselves, and
  'node_heralds' for every other herald, keyed by the atom it renders.
  The server embeds the file at compile time and serves the herald rule
  table with each rule's style taken from 'node_heralds'; it refuses to
  start if the file names a style it does not define, or if a styled
  rule has no entry. In Emacs the
  style's face is 'heralds-STYLE-face' (for example 'heralds-high-face');
  customize it with 'customize-face'. In Neovim its highlight group is
  'SkgHeraldSTYLE' (for example 'SkgHeraldHigh'); override it with
  ':highlight' or 'vim.api.nvim_set_hl' after Skg loads. The Neovim client
  assumes 'termguicolors', since the styles are given as RGB colors.
